WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.020
Have you ever gotten a paper cut, gone about your week, and looked down a few days later

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:07.580
to see it completely healed?

00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:10.780
How exactly did your body know to do that?

00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:13.180
That cellular repair doesn't happen by accident.

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:16.820
It is directed by natural signaling proteins.

00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:21.840
You can think of them as tiny, highly specific messengers carrying instructions, telling

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:26.860
your cells exactly when to calm inflammation and how to rebuild the tissue.

00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:32.360
When we get sick and need outside help, we don't usually turn to those natural messengers.

00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:38.480
For over a hundred years, modern medicine has relied almost entirely on synthetic chemistry.

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:42.620
We invent completely new, artificial molecules in a laboratory.

00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:48.160
These chemicals are built from scratch, entirely outside of the delicate, natural biochemical

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:51.540
language your body uses to communicate with itself.

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:55.820
We have essentially treated the human body like a simple machine with mechanical switches,

00:00:55.820 --> 00:01:00.200
ignoring the rich, coordinated language of healing it already speaks.

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:06.440
Looking at this diagram, legacy chemical pharmacology uses a man-made chemical to forcefully lock

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:09.060
onto a single biological receptor.

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:11.720
But your body is an interconnected network.

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:16.720
When you forcefully pin down just one switch, the surrounding systems notice.

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:20.380
The network reacts, trying to route around the blockage.

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:24.960
As the body fights back against this forced chemical command, patients build tolerance

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:27.900
and often experience severe side effects.

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:32.700
We see the starkest cost of treating human biology this way in the opioid crisis, where

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:38.680
forcefully blocking pain pathways causes the nervous system to heavily adapt and resist.

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:43.700
Whenever a synthetic drug aggressively coerces an isolated part of your biology, the rest

00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:48.220
of your dynamic network will inevitably push back to regain balance.

00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:52.900
This brings us to a completely different approach, called Instructional Medicine.

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:58.400
Instead of inventing new foreign chemicals to force the body into submission, this method

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:03.680
captures the natural biologics your body already produces to heal itself.

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:09.120
Rather than isolating one single target, Instructional Medicine looks at the whole picture.

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:14.420
It gathers entire, coordinated teams of these signaling proteins that naturally work together

00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:19.600
in harmony to achieve a goal, like reducing swelling or repairing tissue.

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:25.260
The specific group of signals your body relies on to direct this repair is known as the Thermosensitive

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:26.760
Peptidome.

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:31.420
Think of this collection like a detailed repair manual, providing the exact instructions

00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:33.340
your cells need to heal.

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:38.140
To actually restore balance, the body needs this complete set of biological instructions

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:43.780
acting together, rather than a single chemical hammer trying to control the entire system.

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:48.420
If our own bodies make the perfect medicine, you might wonder why we haven't simply

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:52.300
collected these natural proteins and used them as therapies before.

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:55.480
The answer comes down to how we manufacture drugs.

00:02:55.480 --> 00:03:01.720
To make medicine safe, traditional pharmaceutical factories rely on extreme heat for sterilization.

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:06.720
Heat is harmless to a rugged synthetic chemical, but it is devastating to the fragile biology

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:08.420
of natural proteins.

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:13.660
A protein's ability to send a message depends entirely on its precise, three-dimensional

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:15.260
folded shape.

00:03:15.260 --> 00:03:18.460
When you apply high heat, that delicate shape unravels.

00:03:18.460 --> 00:03:24.140
The protein melts into a useless string, and the biological instruction is completely destroyed.

00:03:24.140 --> 00:03:29.260
To solve this, a company called Instructional Biology engineered a completely new way to

00:03:29.260 --> 00:03:30.260
make medicine.

00:03:30.260 --> 00:03:35.280
They built a system from the ground up to isolate these fragile proteins and clear away

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:39.780
harmful pathogens without ever using high-temperature sterilization.

00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:44.620
Looking at the right side of this diagram, you can see their connected manufacturing stack.

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:49.420
Step 1 shows how the system gently extracts the native proteins without altering their

00:03:49.420 --> 00:03:51.020
delicate structure.

00:03:51.020 --> 00:03:56.740
Step 2 then uses a specialized, light-based technology to safely clear away pathogens without

00:03:56.740 --> 00:03:59.300
applying any harmful heat.

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:03.300
Because these natural proteins are kept safely away from extreme temperatures, they retain

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:05.700
their complex, folded shapes.

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:10.020
The crucial messages they carry survive intact, from the moment they are discovered

00:04:10.020 --> 00:04:13.220
all the way to the moment they are given to a patient.

00:04:13.220 --> 00:04:18.300
By removing heat from the entire manufacturing pipeline, we can finally collect, preserve,

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:22.380
and use the body's own complex biological software.

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:26.800
This table lays out exactly how a century of synthetic drugs compares to the future

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:28.780
of Instructional Medicine.

00:04:28.780 --> 00:04:33.420
In the first row, we move away from pushing one man-made molecule onto a single target

00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:37.740
and instead use the body's own signaling proteins, which are designed to work together

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:38.980
as a network.

00:04:39.140 --> 00:04:44.140
Next, we stop relying on a fixed dose, calculated for a statistical average patient.

00:04:44.140 --> 00:04:49.460
Instead, dosing is tailored precisely to the individual, monitored safely within a doctor's

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:50.460
limits.

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:55.140
And critically, as shown in the third row, we abandon manufacturing processes that use

00:04:55.140 --> 00:04:59.100
heat and destroy the very shape that makes the medicine work.

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:04.160
The new standard keeps every protein perfectly folded and safely verified.

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:07.600
The most capable pharmacy has been inside of us the entire time.

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:12.040
We just needed the right end-to-end technology to carefully extract those signals, preserve

00:05:12.040 --> 00:05:15.200
them, and deliver them back where they are needed.

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:20.000
The human body operates on a rich, dynamic language of biological instructions.

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:24.320
By learning to capture and speak that language, we are finally creating medicine made by the

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:25.640
body for the body.
